{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AQaWggXLghdC8L7Koba9Fh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TYqUKr63gVoHwaAhyiadvs","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Does Subgroup-Stratified Correction Matter for LLM-Annotated Heterogeneous Treatment Effects?\"\n\n**Core idea:** LLM annotations used as effect modifiers in HTE analysis have subgroup-varying misclassification rates. Applying global confusion-matrix correction may introduce systematic bias. The paper formalizes this bias, derives an analytic bound (bias ∝ Δπ × Δτ), proposes EC-HTE (hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction), and finds empirically that current frontier LLMs rarely trigger conditions where stratified correction matters.\n\n**Three contributions:**\n1. Analytic bound: |Bias_k^global| ≤ C · Δπ · Δτ for K=2 (R²=0.92), conservative for K>2 (R² drops to 0.68/0.52 for K=4/8)\n2. EC-HTE framework: HB Dirichlet partial pooling for per-subgroup confusion matrices + CATE correction via matrix inversion\n3. Negative empirical finding: current frontier LLMs show limited misclassification heterogeneity (max/min 1.10–1.18), making global correction sufficient in most cases\n\n## Related Work as Presented (§2)\n\n**Measurement error in causal inference:** Carroll et al. (2006), Gustafson (2004) on mismeasured covariates. Hu and Schennach (2008), Schennach (2016) on nonparametric identification under misclassification — population-level, not the specific interaction between misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity.\n\n**LLM annotation correction:** \n- PPI (Angelopoulos et al., 2023): valid inference combining LLM + small labeled data, assumes uniform correction\n- DSL (Egami et al., 2024): design-based guarantees for text-as-treatment\n- Mozer (2026): arm-specific rectifiers for differential prediction error, but ATE only\n- PPCI (Sui et al., 2026): kernel localization for conditional functionals, outcome-side measurement error\n\n**HTE methods:** Athey et al. (2019), Wager and Athey (2018) causal forests. ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression forests but not mismeasured effect modifier.\n\n**Bayesian annotation:** Dawid and Skene (1979), Paun et al. (2018) — applied to consensus labeling, not causal estimation.\n\n## Key Experimental Findings (for assessing claim novelty)\n\n- Bias bound: K=2 is exact and elegant (R²=0.92, tightness 1.15), but K>2 degrades significantly (tightness ratio 2.3–3.8). The Δπ×Δτ product is a first-order approximation for K>2.\n- EC-HTE vs PPCI: Methods are \"complementary but partially overlapping.\" PPCI achieves 55% bias reduction on covariate-side error via kernel localization, partially addressing EC-HTE's target problem.\n- Diagnostic criterion: AUC=0.70 — better than chance but insufficient for reliable use. Framed as \"conservative sufficient condition.\"\n- Negative result: All three frontier LLMs (GPT-4.1, Qwen3-235B, Claude Sonnet 4.6) show limited heterogeneity. Negative correlation r=−0.85 suggests problem diminishes as LLMs improve.\n- Budget: EC-HTE needs n≥750 expert labels (K=2 moderate) to outperform global correction.\n- Robustness: EC-HTE becomes worse than global at ≥25% subgroup noise or with misaligned subgroups.\n- Causal forest integration: EC-HTE leaf-level correction underperforms global correction (RMSE 0.038 vs 0.036).\n\n## 12 References in the Paper\nAngelopoulos et al. 2023 (PPI), Ashwin et al. 2025 (refugee data), Athey et al. 2019 (GRF), Bu et al. 2023 (ForestIV), Carroll et al. 2006 (measurement error), Dawid & Skene 1979 (annotator models), Egami et al. 2024 (DSL), Gustafson 2004 (measurement error), Hu & Schennach 2008, Mozer 2026, Paun et al. 2018, Schennach 2016, Sui et al. 2026 (PPCI), Wager & Athey 2018, Ziems et al. 2024.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: Is the bias formalization (Δπ×Δτ bound) genuinely new? Is EC-HTE technically novel beyond standard Bayesian confusion matrix modeling + matrix inversion? Is the negative finding surprising or already expected?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there missing baselines or related works? Especially in measurement error literature, HTE estimation, and CSS annotation quality.\n3. **Positioning**: Is the paper correctly positioned relative to PPI, DSL, PPCI, Mozer? Does the differentiation hold up given PPCI's partial effectiveness on covariate error?\n4. **Significance**: For the CL+CSS community — does this negative finding have sufficient practical value?\n5. **Framing quality**: Is the negative-result framing convincing? Does the paper deliver on what it promises?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n- Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The paper is framed as a negative result — assess whether the negative finding itself constitutes a sufficient contribution.","model":"sonnet"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":761,"cache_read_input_tokens":53359,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":761},"output_tokens":29,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d4a6cb8-7b88-4571-bf20-c9431ac3715e","uuid":"0b7b8109-caeb-42cd-ac1c-580b7bd1a0c7"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a28e9590998bd5004","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TYqUKr63gVoHwaAhyiadvs","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Does Subgroup-Stratified Correction Matter for LLM-Annotated Heterogeneous Treatment Effects?\"\n\n**Core idea:** LLM annotations used as effect modifiers in HTE analysis have subgroup-varying misclassification rates. Applying global confusion-matrix correction may introduce systematic bias. The paper formalizes this bias, derives an analytic bound (bias ∝ Δπ × Δτ), proposes EC-HTE (hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction), and finds empirically that current frontier LLMs rarely trigger conditions where stratified correction matters.\n\n**Three contributions:**\n1. Analytic bound: |Bias_k^global| ≤ C · Δπ · Δτ for K=2 (R²=0.92), conservative for K>2 (R² drops to 0.68/0.52 for K=4/8)\n2. EC-HTE framework: HB Dirichlet partial pooling for per-subgroup confusion matrices + CATE correction via matrix inversion\n3. Negative empirical finding: current frontier LLMs show limited misclassification heterogeneity (max/min 1.10–1.18), making global correction sufficient in most cases\n\n## Related Work as Presented (§2)\n\n**Measurement error in causal inference:** Carroll et al. (2006), Gustafson (2004) on mismeasured covariates. Hu and Schennach (2008), Schennach (2016) on nonparametric identification under misclassification — population-level, not the specific interaction between misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity.\n\n**LLM annotation correction:** \n- PPI (Angelopoulos et al., 2023): valid inference combining LLM + small labeled data, assumes uniform correction\n- DSL (Egami et al., 2024): design-based guarantees for text-as-treatment\n- Mozer (2026): arm-specific rectifiers for differential prediction error, but ATE only\n- PPCI (Sui et al., 2026): kernel localization for conditional functionals, outcome-side measurement error\n\n**HTE methods:** Athey et al. (2019), Wager and Athey (2018) causal forests. ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression forests but not mismeasured effect modifier.\n\n**Bayesian annotation:** Dawid and Skene (1979), Paun et al. (2018) — applied to consensus labeling, not causal estimation.\n\n## Key Experimental Findings (for assessing claim novelty)\n\n- Bias bound: K=2 is exact and elegant (R²=0.92, tightness 1.15), but K>2 degrades significantly (tightness ratio 2.3–3.8). The Δπ×Δτ product is a first-order approximation for K>2.\n- EC-HTE vs PPCI: Methods are \"complementary but partially overlapping.\" PPCI achieves 55% bias reduction on covariate-side error via kernel localization, partially addressing EC-HTE's target problem.\n- Diagnostic criterion: AUC=0.70 — better than chance but insufficient for reliable use. Framed as \"conservative sufficient condition.\"\n- Negative result: All three frontier LLMs (GPT-4.1, Qwen3-235B, Claude Sonnet 4.6) show limited heterogeneity. Negative correlation r=−0.85 suggests problem diminishes as LLMs improve.\n- Budget: EC-HTE needs n≥750 expert labels (K=2 moderate) to outperform global correction.\n- Robustness: EC-HTE becomes worse than global at ≥25% subgroup noise or with misaligned subgroups.\n- Causal forest integration: EC-HTE leaf-level correction underperforms global correction (RMSE 0.038 vs 0.036).\n\n## 12 References in the Paper\nAngelopoulos et al. 2023 (PPI), Ashwin et al. 2025 (refugee data), Athey et al. 2019 (GRF), Bu et al. 2023 (ForestIV), Carroll et al. 2006 (measurement error), Dawid & Skene 1979 (annotator models), Egami et al. 2024 (DSL), Gustafson 2004 (measurement error), Hu & Schennach 2008, Mozer 2026, Paun et al. 2018, Schennach 2016, Sui et al. 2026 (PPCI), Wager & Athey 2018, Ziems et al. 2024.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: Is the bias formalization (Δπ×Δτ bound) genuinely new? Is EC-HTE technically novel beyond standard Bayesian confusion matrix modeling + matrix inversion? Is the negative finding surprising or already expected?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there missing baselines or related works? Especially in measurement error literature, HTE estimation, and CSS annotation quality.\n3. **Positioning**: Is the paper correctly positioned relative to PPI, DSL, PPCI, Mozer? Does the differentiation hold up given PPCI's partial effectiveness on covariate error?\n4. **Significance**: For the CL+CSS community — does this negative finding have sufficient practical value?\n5. **Framing quality**: Is the negative-result framing convincing? Does the paper deliver on what it promises?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n- Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The paper is framed as a negative result — assess whether the negative finding itself constitutes a sufficient contribution.","uuid":"ac134662-54fd-43f9-85bb-73a615777f20","session_id":"7d4a6cb8-7b88-4571-bf20-c9431ac3715e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Does Subgroup-Stratified Correction Matter for LLM-Annotated Heterogeneous Treatment Effects?\"\n\n**Core idea:** LLM annotations used as effect modifiers in HTE analysis have subgroup-varying misclassification rates. Applying global confusion-matrix correction may introduce systematic bias. The paper formalizes this bias, derives an analytic bound (bias ∝ Δπ × Δτ), proposes EC-HTE (hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction), and finds empirically that current frontier LLMs rarely trigger conditions where stratified correction matters.\n\n**Three contributions:**\n1. Analytic bound: |Bias_k^global| ≤ C · Δπ · Δτ for K=2 (R²=0.92), conservative for K>2 (R² drops to 0.68/0.52 for K=4/8)\n2. EC-HTE framework: HB Dirichlet partial pooling for per-subgroup confusion matrices + CATE correction via matrix inversion\n3. Negative empirical finding: current frontier LLMs show limited misclassification heterogeneity (max/min 1.10–1.18), making global correction sufficient in most cases\n\n## Related Work as Presented (§2)\n\n**Measurement error in causal inference:** Carroll et al. (2006), Gustafson (2004) on mismeasured covariates. Hu and Schennach (2008), Schennach (2016) on nonparametric identification under misclassification — population-level, not the specific interaction between misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity.\n\n**LLM annotation correction:** \n- PPI (Angelopoulos et al., 2023): valid inference combining LLM + small labeled data, assumes uniform correction\n- DSL (Egami et al., 2024): design-based guarantees for text-as-treatment\n- Mozer (2026): arm-specific rectifiers for differential prediction error, but ATE only\n- PPCI (Sui et al., 2026): kernel localization for conditional functionals, outcome-side measurement error\n\n**HTE methods:** Athey et al. (2019), Wager and Athey (2018) causal forests. ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression forests but not mismeasured effect modifier.\n\n**Bayesian annotation:** Dawid and Skene (1979), Paun et al. (2018) — applied to consensus labeling, not causal estimation.\n\n## Key Experimental Findings (for assessing claim novelty)\n\n- Bias bound: K=2 is exact and elegant (R²=0.92, tightness 1.15), but K>2 degrades significantly (tightness ratio 2.3–3.8). The Δπ×Δτ product is a first-order approximation for K>2.\n- EC-HTE vs PPCI: Methods are \"complementary but partially overlapping.\" PPCI achieves 55% bias reduction on covariate-side error via kernel localization, partially addressing EC-HTE's target problem.\n- Diagnostic criterion: AUC=0.70 — better than chance but insufficient for reliable use. Framed as \"conservative sufficient condition.\"\n- Negative result: All three frontier LLMs (GPT-4.1, Qwen3-235B, Claude Sonnet 4.6) show limited heterogeneity. Negative correlation r=−0.85 suggests problem diminishes as LLMs improve.\n- Budget: EC-HTE needs n≥750 expert labels (K=2 moderate) to outperform global correction.\n- Robustness: EC-HTE becomes worse than global at ≥25% subgroup noise or with misaligned subgroups.\n- Causal forest integration: EC-HTE leaf-level correction underperforms global correction (RMSE 0.038 vs 0.036).\n\n## 12 References in the Paper\nAngelopoulos et al. 2023 (PPI), Ashwin et al. 2025 (refugee data), Athey et al. 2019 (GRF), Bu et al. 2023 (ForestIV), Carroll et al. 2006 (measurement error), Dawid & Skene 1979 (annotator models), Egami et al. 2024 (DSL), Gustafson 2004 (measurement error), Hu & Schennach 2008, Mozer 2026, Paun et al. 2018, Schennach 2016, Sui et al. 2026 (PPCI), Wager & Athey 2018, Ziems et al. 2024.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: Is the bias formalization (Δπ×Δτ bound) genuinely new? Is EC-HTE technically novel beyond standard Bayesian confusion matrix modeling + matrix inversion? Is the negative finding surprising or already expected?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there missing baselines or related works? Especially in measurement error literature, HTE estimation, and CSS annotation quality.\n3. **Positioning**: Is the paper correctly positioned relative to PPI, DSL, PPCI, Mozer? Does the differentiation hold up given PPCI's partial effectiveness on covariate error?\n4. **Significance**: For the CL+CSS community — does this negative finding have sufficient practical value?\n5. **Framing quality**: Is the negative-result framing convincing? Does the paper deliver on what it promises?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n- Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The paper is framed as a negative result — assess whether the negative finding itself constitutes a sufficient contribution."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TYqUKr63gVoHwaAhyiadvs","session_id":"7d4a6cb8-7b88-4571-bf20-c9431ac3715e","uuid":"76e3c2e3-e33d-40c8-972f-e1b0d98f10b4","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:34:17.473Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ab30bd61a82d7d149","tool_use_id":"toolu_014DqNaKZHEWyCkeC7bDNNjz","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":16217,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":57712},"uuid":"d2c18a02-54fd-40ee-b133-cf8e0fb7464e","session_id":"7d4a6cb8-7b88-4571-bf20-c9431ac3715e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014DqNaKZHEWyCkeC7bDNNjz","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Review: \"When Does Subgroup-Stratified Correction Matter for LLM-Annotated Heterogeneous Treatment Effects?\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates whether subgroup-stratified confusion-matrix correction is necessary when using LLM annotations as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect estimation. The central contribution is a formalized bias bound (|Bias| ≤ C · Δπ · Δτ) and the EC-HTE hierarchical Bayesian framework for stratified correction, supported by a comprehensive 11-experiment evaluation spanning synthetic, semi-synthetic, and real data. The main finding is negative: frontier LLMs exhibit sufficiently low misclassification heterogeneity that global correction's residual bias is modest, and EC-HTE provides only marginal gains at substantial computational and data cost.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Rigorous formalization of an underexplored problem.** The bias decomposition in Proposition 1 cleanly separates the contributions of misclassification heterogeneity (Δπ) and treatment effect heterogeneity (Δτ), providing a principled diagnostic criterion that practitioners can actually compute before committing to the more expensive stratified approach.\n\n- **Honest negative result with quantified effect sizes.** The paper does not retreat to \"more research needed\" language. It reports concrete amplification factors (1.05–1.15×), max/min ratios (1.10–1.18), and bias reductions (22–38%) that allow readers to assess practical significance. The negative correlation r=−0.85 between LLM quality and misclassification heterogeneity is a particularly clean empirical regularity.\n\n- **Comprehensive experiment coverage.** All 11 pre-registered experiments are reported, including adversarial robustness (exp-010, showing EC-HTE degrades under ≥25% subgroup noise), the PPCI comparison revealing complementary error-type coverage (exp-007), and causal forest integration (exp-011). The synthetic-to-real progression is methodologically appropriate.\n\n- **EC-HTE design is internally coherent.** The Dirichlet partial-pooling prior is the correct choice for confusion-matrix rows, and the hierarchical structure is a principled response to the small-n-per-subgroup problem. Reporting MCMC diagnostics (R̂_max=1.005, ESS≥210) shows appropriate attention to posterior quality.\n\n- **Practical contribution of the diagnostic criterion.** Even if EC-HTE rarely beats global correction in the tested regimes, the Δπ × Δτ product as a screening tool has immediate applied value and is a cleaner takeaway than the framework itself.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**Major:**\n\n- **Bound tightness degrades catastrophically for K>2.** R² drops from 0.92 (K=2) to 0.68 (K=4) and 0.52 (K=8), with the tightness ratio reaching 3.80× at K=8. For applications with more than two annotation categories — which are common in political science and social science HTE studies (e.g., multi-category treatment moderators) — the K=2 bound provides almost no reliable diagnostic guidance. The paper defends this as a \"sufficient condition,\" but a 3.80× loose sufficient condition is clinically uninformative. The paper should either derive a tighter multi-category bound or explicitly restrict claims to K=2 settings.\n\n- **Coverage systematically below nominal (83–91% vs. 95%) with no satisfactory explanation.** This is a fundamental failure of the Bayesian credible interval machinery, not a minor calibration issue. For a framework whose practical value partly rests on uncertainty quantification (HB over MLE), miscoverage of this magnitude undermines the advertised benefit. The paper reports the diagnostic numbers but does not trace the root cause: is this prior misspecification, MCMC approximation error, the inverse-confusion-matrix step introducing non-Gaussian posterior geometry, or a theoretical problem with the credible-to-confidence interval mapping? Without a diagnosis, readers cannot know whether this is fixable or structural.\n\n- **The real-data evaluation (exp-004) lacks a validity anchor.** Table 9 shows EC-HTE shifts CATEs by up to 0.035, but with no ground-truth CATEs available, it is impossible to determine whether these shifts represent bias reduction or overcorrection. The paper acknowledges this but offers no partial-identification bounds, sensitivity analysis, or placebo test to provide even indirect evidence. A real-data section that cannot distinguish signal from noise provides limited evidentiary value and should either be dropped or supplemented with a design-based sensitivity analysis.\n\n- **HB underperforms MLE on RMSE (+5.5% at K=4 extreme regime) with 45× runtime cost.** The theoretical motivation for HB (partial pooling for small n) predicts it should dominate MLE in the small-sample regime. That it is worse in the extreme heterogeneity case at K=4 is a theoretically concerning result that the paper does not adequately explain. If partial pooling actively hurts when heterogeneity is real and large — exactly the regime where stratified correction would matter most — this is a meaningful limitation of the framework's applicability.\n\n**Minor:**\n\n- **Budget sensitivity crossovers are concerning for practical use.** For K≥4 moderate heterogeneity, the crossover point exceeds n=2000 per subgroup, which is prohibitive in many applied settings. The paper reports this but does not translate it into clear guidance about when EC-HTE is feasible. A simple decision table (K × heterogeneity regime × required n) would substantially improve usability.\n\n- **The adaptive allocation result (exp-009, 12% RMSE at n=250) is presented as a robustness check but may deserve more attention.** If annotation budgets are constrained, adaptive allocation appears to be the highest-leverage practical intervention, outperforming the correction framework itself. The paper somewhat undersells this.\n\n- **exp-010 shows 31% degradation with misaligned subgroups but does not characterize the practical frequency of such misalignment.** In real applications where LLM-defined and analyst-defined subgroups differ, this is a deployment-critical failure mode. A brief empirical characterization of how common this scenario is in the real-data setting would strengthen the robustness section.\n\n- **The diagnostic criterion AUC=0.70 with sensitivity=0.52 at FPR=0.10 is underanalyzed.** The paper correctly identifies this as \"insufficient for reliable decision-making,\" but does not explore whether the criterion's performance varies systematically with K, n, or the magnitude of Δτ. Given that this criterion is arguably the most immediately practical output of the paper, its failure deserves a more thorough post-mortem.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the K>2 bound:** For K=8, the 3.80× tightness ratio means the bound may flag settings as \"safe to use global correction\" when the actual bias is nearly 4× the bound threshold. Can you provide an empirical characterization of the false-negative rate of the diagnostic criterion specifically for K>2? Is there a tighter bound available for specific confusion matrix structures (e.g., symmetric misclassification)?\n\n2. **On coverage failure:** Have you investigated whether the 83–91% coverage is attributable to the inverse confusion matrix step specifically? Propagating uncertainty through a matrix inversion can produce heavy-tailed posteriors that credible intervals undercover. Did you compare coverage with and without this step (e.g., using simulated data where the true confusion matrix is known)?\n\n3. **On HB worse than MLE at K=4 extreme:** Theoretically, partial pooling should only help when subgroups share information. If the extreme heterogeneity regime (by definition) means subgroups have very different confusion matrices, is the HB prior's shared hyperprior actually inducing harmful shrinkage? Did you examine the posterior hyperparameter estimates in this regime?\n\n4. **On the real data:** Is there any subset of the Ashwin dataset where a natural experiment or placebo test could be constructed to validate the direction of EC-HTE's corrections — for example, a subgroup where annotation quality is independently verified or where a different measurement instrument is available?\n\n5. **On the r=−0.85 regularity:** This is an interesting finding about LLM quality and heterogeneity, but is it robust to the choice of LLMs? Three models is a thin basis for a correlation. Did you consider including any open-source models with more variable annotation quality (e.g., smaller Llama variants) that might populate a different region of the quality-heterogeneity space?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n- **A simulation study explicitly targeting the K>2 bound failure regime** would be necessary to understand when the sufficient condition misleads. A 2×2 table showing false-negative rates of the diagnostic criterion as a function of K and heterogeneity magnitude would directly address the main gap.\n\n- **Posterior predictive checks for the HB model.** Given the coverage failure, reporting whether the HB model's posterior predictive distribution matches observed confusion matrix frequencies across subgroups is essential for diagnosing whether the issue is prior misspecification.\n\n- **A comparison of EC-HTE against a simpler frequentist stratified correction with bootstrap confidence intervals.** The paper compares HB vs. MLE-stratified, but a bootstrap-corrected MLE might close most of the coverage gap at far less computational cost. This comparison is needed to justify the MCMC overhead.\n\n- **Sensitivity of the real-data conclusions to subgroup definition.** The paper uses analyst-specified subgroups, but the exp-010 result shows EC-HTE is sensitive to subgroup misalignment. An analysis of how the Ashwin results change under alternative subgroup definitions would assess the robustness of the (already tentative) real-data findings.\n\n---\n\n### Score and Justification\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe paper addresses a genuine and underexplored problem at the intersection of causal inference and LLM annotation, and the honest negative result is a legitimate scientific contribution. The experimental design is unusually thorough, and the bias decomposition is clean for K=2.\n\nHowever, three issues together prevent acceptance. First, the K>2 bound failure (R²=0.52, tightness 3.80×) is not adequately resolved — the paper's primary analytical contribution loses most of its diagnostic value for realistic multi-category settings. Second, the systematic coverage failure (83–91% vs. 95%) in the HB framework is undiagnosed; for a paper that proposes HB as the primary correction method, unexplained miscoverage is a methodological red flag. Third, the real-data evaluation, while appreciated, provides essentially no epistemic leverage without a validity anchor, yet it occupies substantial space and is cited as supporting evidence.\n\nThe paper is closer to acceptance than rejection. With a thorough treatment of the coverage issue (root cause identified and either fixed or explicitly bounded), a tighter characterization of the K>2 regime, and a clearer framing of the real-data section as illustrative rather than confirmatory, this would be a clean Findings-range paper. As currently written, the gap between the methodological claims and the methodological evidence is too large.\n\n**Confidence: 4** (familiar with causal inference methods and LLM evaluation methodology; limited expertise in hierarchical Bayesian misclassification correction specifically)"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab30bd61a82d7d149 (use SendMessage with to: 'ab30bd61a82d7d149' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 18873\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 57722</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d4a6cb8-7b88-4571-bf20-c9431ac3715e","uuid":"cfbcb336-eb8a-44d3-b358-2927a6631543","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:34:46.496Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Methods and Experiments**. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Does Subgroup-Stratified Correction Matter for LLM-Annotated Heterogeneous Treatment Effects?\"\n\n**Core idea:** LLM annotations used as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect (HTE) analysis have subgroup-varying misclassification rates. Applying a global (pooled) confusion-matrix correction may introduce systematic bias. The paper formalizes this bias mechanism, derives an analytic bound (bias ∝ Δπ × Δτ), proposes EC-HTE (a hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction framework), and evaluates it across synthetic, semi-synthetic, and real data with three frontier LLMs. The central finding is **negative**: current frontier LLMs exhibit limited misclassification heterogeneity (max/min ratios 1.10–1.18), global correction's additional bias is small (amplification 1.05–1.15×), and EC-HTE provides only modest improvement (22–38% relative bias reduction) at substantial cost.\n\n**Three stated contributions:**\n1. Analytic bound for bias from global correction under heterogeneous misclassification (K=2 tight R²=0.92; K>2 conservative sufficient condition)\n2. EC-HTE framework (HB Dirichlet partial pooling for subgroup-stratified confusion matrices)\n3. Negative empirical finding: current LLMs rarely trigger conditions where stratified correction is necessary\n\n## Paper Structure and Key Results\n\n**§3 Bias Mechanism (exp-002):**\n- Proposition 1: |Bias_k^global| ≤ C · Δπ · Δτ for K=2\n- MC verification: R²=0.92 for K=2, degrades to 0.68 (K=4), 0.52 (K=8)\n- Bound tightness ratio: 1.15 (K=2), 2.30 (K=4), 3.80 (K=8)\n- At realistic Δπ∈[0.05,0.10], amplification only 1.08–1.18×\n\n**§4 EC-HTE Framework:**\n- MLE stratified: per-subgroup confusion matrix estimation\n- HB: Dirichlet prior on confusion matrix rows, Gamma hyperprior, MCMC via NUTS\n- Corrected CATE via inverse confusion matrix: p_corrected = (Π̂^(k))^{-1} p_observed\n- Diagnostic criterion: Δπ × Δτ product as conservative sufficient condition\n\n**§5.1.1 HB vs MLE (exp-001):**\n- Table 3: RMSE differences negligible across regimes. HB slightly worse for K=4 (+5.5% extreme, +2.1% moderate)\n- Coverage systematically below nominal (83–91% vs 95%)\n- MCMC diagnostics OK (R̂_max=1.005, ESS≥210). Runtime 45× MLE.\n\n**§5.1.2 Budget Sensitivity (exp-006):**\n- Table 5: Crossover at n=750 for K=2 moderate, n=350 for K=2 extreme\n- K≥4 moderate: crossover >2000 (never reached)\n- EC-HTE needs substantial per-subgroup labels for K×K confusion matrix estimation\n\n**§5.2.1 Semi-Synthetic (exp-003):**\n- Table 6: GPT-4.1 max/min=1.12, Qwen3=1.18\n- Table 7: EC-HTE max bias 0.011 vs global 0.015 (GPT-4.1); 0.017 vs 0.028 (Qwen3)\n- Absolute improvements small (largest gap = 0.011)\n\n**§5.2.2 Ashwin Real Data (exp-004):**\n- Table 8: max/min ratio 1.22 (higher than semi-synthetic)\n- Table 9: EC-HTE shifts CATEs by up to 0.035, but no qualitative conclusion changes\n- No ground-truth CATEs → cannot determine if EC-HTE corrections are genuine improvement or overcorrection\n\n**§5.2.3 Multi-LLM (exp-008):**\n- Table 10: All three LLMs show modest heterogeneity (1.07–1.18)\n- Table 11: Qwen3 on \"refugee×female×low edu\" subgroup: accuracy 0.71 (n=85, SE=0.049)\n- Negative correlation r=−0.85 between LLM quality and heterogeneity\n\n**§5.3.1 EC-HTE vs PPCI (exp-007):**\n- Table 12: EC-HTE excels on covariate-side error (0.08 vs PPCI 0.45), PPCI excels on outcome-side (0.05 vs 0.42)\n- PPCI achieves partial effectiveness on covariate error (0.45 vs 1.0 no-correction = 55% reduction)\n- Composition yields 0.22 (both errors), modest non-additive improvement\n\n**§5.3.2 Diagnostic (exp-005):**\n- Table 13: Overall AUC=0.70, sensitivity@FPR=0.10 only 0.52\n- Insufficient for reliable decision-making\n\n**§5.4.1 Robustness (exp-010):**\n- Table 14: EC-HTE worse than global at ≥25% subgroup noise, 31% worse with misaligned subgroups\n- HB partial pooling partially mitigates but insufficient\n\n**§5.4.2 Adaptive Allocation (exp-009):**\n- Table 15: 12% RMSE improvement at n=250, diminishes to 4% at n=1000\n- Useful practical tip, not substantial contribution\n\n**§5.4.3 Causal Forest (exp-011):**\n- Table 16: EC-HTE leaf-level RMSE 0.038 > global 0.036; convergence rate 0.78\n- Subgroup-level slightly better (0.037) but no clear win over global\n\n## Experiment Design Reference\nAll 11 imagined experiments (exp-001 through exp-011) are covered in the paper. The paper reports all experiments from the design document.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Methodological soundness** of the bias bound derivation and EC-HTE framework\n2. **Experimental rigor**: design, statistical validity, coverage of the design space\n3. **Reproducibility** concerns\n4. **Strengths and weaknesses** of the experimental evaluation\n5. **Coverage**: are the experiments sufficient to support the claims?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Missing Experiments or Analyses\n- Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The paper is framed as a negative result.","agentId":"ab30bd61a82d7d149","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Review: \"When Does Subgroup-Stratified Correction Matter for LLM-Annotated Heterogeneous Treatment Effects?\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates whether subgroup-stratified confusion-matrix correction is necessary when using LLM annotations as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect estimation. The central contribution is a formalized bias bound (|Bias| ≤ C · Δπ · Δτ) and the EC-HTE hierarchical Bayesian framework for stratified correction, supported by a comprehensive 11-experiment evaluation spanning synthetic, semi-synthetic, and real data. The main finding is negative: frontier LLMs exhibit sufficiently low misclassification heterogeneity that global correction's residual bias is modest, and EC-HTE provides only marginal gains at substantial computational and data cost.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Rigorous formalization of an underexplored problem.** The bias decomposition in Proposition 1 cleanly separates the contributions of misclassification heterogeneity (Δπ) and treatment effect heterogeneity (Δτ), providing a principled diagnostic criterion that practitioners can actually compute before committing to the more expensive stratified approach.\n\n- **Honest negative result with quantified effect sizes.** The paper does not retreat to \"more research needed\" language. It reports concrete amplification factors (1.05–1.15×), max/min ratios (1.10–1.18), and bias reductions (22–38%) that allow readers to assess practical significance. The negative correlation r=−0.85 between LLM quality and misclassification heterogeneity is a particularly clean empirical regularity.\n\n- **Comprehensive experiment coverage.** All 11 pre-registered experiments are reported, including adversarial robustness (exp-010, showing EC-HTE degrades under ≥25% subgroup noise), the PPCI comparison revealing complementary error-type coverage (exp-007), and causal forest integration (exp-011). The synthetic-to-real progression is methodologically appropriate.\n\n- **EC-HTE design is internally coherent.** The Dirichlet partial-pooling prior is the correct choice for confusion-matrix rows, and the hierarchical structure is a principled response to the small-n-per-subgroup problem. Reporting MCMC diagnostics (R̂_max=1.005, ESS≥210) shows appropriate attention to posterior quality.\n\n- **Practical contribution of the diagnostic criterion.** Even if EC-HTE rarely beats global correction in the tested regimes, the Δπ × Δτ product as a screening tool has immediate applied value and is a cleaner takeaway than the framework itself.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**Major:**\n\n- **Bound tightness degrades catastrophically for K>2.** R² drops from 0.92 (K=2) to 0.68 (K=4) and 0.52 (K=8), with the tightness ratio reaching 3.80× at K=8. For applications with more than two annotation categories — which are common in political science and social science HTE studies (e.g., multi-category treatment moderators) — the K=2 bound provides almost no reliable diagnostic guidance. The paper defends this as a \"sufficient condition,\" but a 3.80× loose sufficient condition is clinically uninformative. The paper should either derive a tighter multi-category bound or explicitly restrict claims to K=2 settings.\n\n- **Coverage systematically below nominal (83–91% vs. 95%) with no satisfactory explanation.** This is a fundamental failure of the Bayesian credible interval machinery, not a minor calibration issue. For a framework whose practical value partly rests on uncertainty quantification (HB over MLE), miscoverage of this magnitude undermines the advertised benefit. The paper reports the diagnostic numbers but does not trace the root cause: is this prior misspecification, MCMC approximation error, the inverse-confusion-matrix step introducing non-Gaussian posterior geometry, or a theoretical problem with the credible-to-confidence interval mapping? Without a diagnosis, readers cannot know whether this is fixable or structural.\n\n- **The real-data evaluation (exp-004) lacks a validity anchor.** Table 9 shows EC-HTE shifts CATEs by up to 0.035, but with no ground-truth CATEs available, it is impossible to determine whether these shifts represent bias reduction or overcorrection. The paper acknowledges this but offers no partial-identification bounds, sensitivity analysis, or placebo test to provide even indirect evidence. A real-data section that cannot distinguish signal from noise provides limited evidentiary value and should either be dropped or supplemented with a design-based sensitivity analysis.\n\n- **HB underperforms MLE on RMSE (+5.5% at K=4 extreme regime) with 45× runtime cost.** The theoretical motivation for HB (partial pooling for small n) predicts it should dominate MLE in the small-sample regime. That it is worse in the extreme heterogeneity case at K=4 is a theoretically concerning result that the paper does not adequately explain. If partial pooling actively hurts when heterogeneity is real and large — exactly the regime where stratified correction would matter most — this is a meaningful limitation of the framework's applicability.\n\n**Minor:**\n\n- **Budget sensitivity crossovers are concerning for practical use.** For K≥4 moderate heterogeneity, the crossover point exceeds n=2000 per subgroup, which is prohibitive in many applied settings. The paper reports this but does not translate it into clear guidance about when EC-HTE is feasible. A simple decision table (K × heterogeneity regime × required n) would substantially improve usability.\n\n- **The adaptive allocation result (exp-009, 12% RMSE at n=250) is presented as a robustness check but may deserve more attention.** If annotation budgets are constrained, adaptive allocation appears to be the highest-leverage practical intervention, outperforming the correction framework itself. The paper somewhat undersells this.\n\n- **exp-010 shows 31% degradation with misaligned subgroups but does not characterize the practical frequency of such misalignment.** In real applications where LLM-defined and analyst-defined subgroups differ, this is a deployment-critical failure mode. A brief empirical characterization of how common this scenario is in the real-data setting would strengthen the robustness section.\n\n- **The diagnostic criterion AUC=0.70 with sensitivity=0.52 at FPR=0.10 is underanalyzed.** The paper correctly identifies this as \"insufficient for reliable decision-making,\" but does not explore whether the criterion's performance varies systematically with K, n, or the magnitude of Δτ. Given that this criterion is arguably the most immediately practical output of the paper, its failure deserves a more thorough post-mortem.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **On the K>2 bound:** For K=8, the 3.80× tightness ratio means the bound may flag settings as \"safe to use global correction\" when the actual bias is nearly 4× the bound threshold. Can you provide an empirical characterization of the false-negative rate of the diagnostic criterion specifically for K>2? Is there a tighter bound available for specific confusion matrix structures (e.g., symmetric misclassification)?\n\n2. **On coverage failure:** Have you investigated whether the 83–91% coverage is attributable to the inverse confusion matrix step specifically? Propagating uncertainty through a matrix inversion can produce heavy-tailed posteriors that credible intervals undercover. Did you compare coverage with and without this step (e.g., using simulated data where the true confusion matrix is known)?\n\n3. **On HB worse than MLE at K=4 extreme:** Theoretically, partial pooling should only help when subgroups share information. If the extreme heterogeneity regime (by definition) means subgroups have very different confusion matrices, is the HB prior's shared hyperprior actually inducing harmful shrinkage? Did you examine the posterior hyperparameter estimates in this regime?\n\n4. **On the real data:** Is there any subset of the Ashwin dataset where a natural experiment or placebo test could be constructed to validate the direction of EC-HTE's corrections — for example, a subgroup where annotation quality is independently verified or where a different measurement instrument is available?\n\n5. **On the r=−0.85 regularity:** This is an interesting finding about LLM quality and heterogeneity, but is it robust to the choice of LLMs? Three models is a thin basis for a correlation. Did you consider including any open-source models with more variable annotation quality (e.g., smaller Llama variants) that might populate a different region of the quality-heterogeneity space?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analyses\n\n- **A simulation study explicitly targeting the K>2 bound failure regime** would be necessary to understand when the sufficient condition misleads. A 2×2 table showing false-negative rates of the diagnostic criterion as a function of K and heterogeneity magnitude would directly address the main gap.\n\n- **Posterior predictive checks for the HB model.** Given the coverage failure, reporting whether the HB model's posterior predictive distribution matches observed confusion matrix frequencies across subgroups is essential for diagnosing whether the issue is prior misspecification.\n\n- **A comparison of EC-HTE against a simpler frequentist stratified correction with bootstrap confidence intervals.** The paper compares HB vs. MLE-stratified, but a bootstrap-corrected MLE might close most of the coverage gap at far less computational cost. This comparison is needed to justify the MCMC overhead.\n\n- **Sensitivity of the real-data conclusions to subgroup definition.** The paper uses analyst-specified subgroups, but the exp-010 result shows EC-HTE is sensitive to subgroup misalignment. An analysis of how the Ashwin results change under alternative subgroup definitions would assess the robustness of the (already tentative) real-data findings.\n\n---\n\n### Score and Justification\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe paper addresses a genuine and underexplored problem at the intersection of causal inference and LLM annotation, and the honest negative result is a legitimate scientific contribution. The experimental design is unusually thorough, and the bias decomposition is clean for K=2.\n\nHowever, three issues together prevent acceptance. First, the K>2 bound failure (R²=0.52, tightness 3.80×) is not adequately resolved — the paper's primary analytical contribution loses most of its diagnostic value for realistic multi-category settings. Second, the systematic coverage failure (83–91% vs. 95%) in the HB framework is undiagnosed; for a paper that proposes HB as the primary correction method, unexplained miscoverage is a methodological red flag. Third, the real-data evaluation, while appreciated, provides essentially no epistemic leverage without a validity anchor, yet it occupies substantial space and is cited as supporting evidence.\n\nThe paper is closer to acceptance than rejection. With a thorough treatment of the coverage issue (root cause identified and either fixed or explicitly bounded), a tighter characterization of the K>2 regime, and a clearer framing of the real-data section as illustrative rather than confirmatory, this would be a clean Findings-range paper. As currently written, the gap between the methodological claims and the methodological evidence is too large.\n\n**Confidence: 4** (familiar with causal inference methods and LLM evaluation methodology; limited expertise in hierarchical Bayesian misclassification correction specifically)"}],"totalDurationMs":57722,"totalTokens":18873,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16213,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2657,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":16213},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":2657,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16213,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":16213,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a28e9590998bd5004","tool_use_id":"toolu_01TYqUKr63gVoHwaAhyiadvs","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":15956,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":63516},"uuid":"7abe6450-8389-48a9-be2c-af11f52b1905","session_id":"7d4a6cb8-7b88-4571-bf20-c9431ac3715e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TYqUKr63gVoHwaAhyiadvs","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Review: \"When Does Subgroup-Stratified Correction Matter for LLM-Annotated Heterogeneous Treatment Effects?\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThe paper investigates whether subgroup-heterogeneous misclassification rates in LLM annotations introduce meaningful bias when estimating heterogeneous treatment effects (HTE), formalizing a bias bound proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity (Δπ) and CATE heterogeneity (Δτ), proposing a hierarchical Bayesian correction framework (EC-HTE), and ultimately reporting that current frontier LLMs rarely trigger conditions where stratified correction meaningfully outperforms global correction. The central contribution is a negative empirical finding grounded in a theoretical framework, positioned against the growing literature on LLM-assisted causal inference. While the bias formalization is clean and the empirical scope is reasonable, the technical novelty is modest, and the negative finding—though useful—risks being interpreted as a justification for inaction rather than a principled demarcation of when the problem matters.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Clean analytic result for K=2.** The |Bias_k^global| ≤ C · Δπ · Δτ bound is elegant and practically interpretable. An R²=0.92 tightness with ratio 1.15 means the bound is genuinely informative for the binary case, which is the most common case in CSS annotation tasks. This is the paper's strongest contribution.\n\n- **Precise problem formulation.** The paper identifies a specific gap—interaction between misclassification *heterogeneity* and CATE heterogeneity—that is distinct from existing measurement error literature (Carroll/Gustafson) focused on population-level error, and from PPI/PPCI which assume uniform or outcome-side error. The conceptual framing is tight and non-obvious.\n\n- **Honest reporting of negative results.** The authors do not oversell EC-HTE. Reporting that EC-HTE underperforms global correction on causal forest integration (RMSE 0.038 vs 0.036), degrades at ≥25% subgroup noise, and requires n≥750 expert labels is a level of transparency uncommon in ML venues. The negative correlation r=−0.85 between LLM quality and misclassification heterogeneity is a particularly useful calibration for the community.\n\n- **Practical diagnostic value.** The AUC=0.70 sufficient-condition criterion, even if imperfect, provides practitioners a pre-analysis check before committing to expensive stratified annotation. For CSS researchers deploying LLMs at scale, \"when does this even matter?\" is the right prior question.\n\n- **Correct gap identification relative to ForestIV.** ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression forests but does not address the mismeasured *effect modifier* setting. The distinction is correctly drawn and not trivial.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**Major:**\n\n- **K>2 bound degrades severely and this is underplayed.** The tightness ratio of 2.3–3.8 for K=4/8 (R² dropping to 0.68/0.52) means the bound is near-vacuous for multi-class effect modifiers. Many realistic CSS annotation tasks involve ordinal or multi-class labels (e.g., sentiment polarity, issue framing, political leaning). The paper implicitly positions itself as a general framework while delivering a tight result only for binary modifiers. This should be clearly foregrounded as a limitation, not buried. The claim that Δπ×Δτ is a \"first-order approximation for K>2\" requires theoretical justification—what are the higher-order terms, and when do they dominate?\n\n- **EC-HTE novelty is thin.** The framework combines two well-known components: (1) Dawid-Skene-style Dirichlet confusion matrix estimation (Dawid & Skene 1979, Paun et al. 2018) with partial pooling, and (2) CATE correction via confusion matrix inversion. Both components exist in the literature; their combination in a hierarchical Bayesian wrapper is an engineering contribution, not a conceptual one. The paper should more explicitly acknowledge this and argue why the combination creates emergent properties—e.g., uncertainty propagation across the annotation and estimation stages—that would not be achieved by applying each component sequentially.\n\n- **PPCI partial overlap is insufficiently resolved.** The paper concedes PPCI achieves 55% bias reduction on covariate-side error via kernel localization and is \"complementary but partially overlapping.\" This framing is evasive. The critical question is: under what conditions does EC-HTE strictly dominate PPCI, and vice versa? Without a formal or empirical regime map (e.g., varying the fraction of error that is covariate-side vs. effect-modifier-side), the reader cannot assess whether EC-HTE fills a genuine gap or a narrow residual one. This is not a minor positioning issue—it goes to the core claim of the paper's necessity.\n\n- **The negative finding may be dataset-specific rather than generalizable.** The empirical result that frontier LLMs show max/min ratios of 1.10–1.18 is reported for three LLMs on what appears to be a single refugee-policy dataset (Ashwin et al., 2025). The finding that misclassification heterogeneity is limited could reflect properties of this particular annotation task and subgroup structure rather than a general law. The r=−0.85 correlation is intriguing but estimated over three data points (three LLMs). Reporting this correlation as if it characterizes a trend is statistically fragile. Broader empirical validation across annotation tasks with known ground-truth structure (e.g., social science datasets with high annotator disagreement, political or medical text) is needed to make the negative finding a genuine contribution rather than a case study.\n\n**Minor:**\n\n- **Diagnostic criterion underperformance not fully addressed.** An AUC of 0.70 for the sufficient condition is labeled \"conservative,\" but no baseline is given. A random subgroup-stratification detector would yield AUC=0.50; the relevant comparison is to simpler heuristics (e.g., subgroup size imbalance, annotation confidence variance). Without this, it is unclear how much the diagnostic adds over simple data inspection.\n\n- **Reference list is thin and potentially incomplete.** Twelve references for a paper targeting a top venue is sparse. The measurement error literature has directly relevant work beyond Carroll/Gustafson/Hu/Schennach—specifically, Chen et al. (2008) on semiparametric efficiency with mismeasured regressors, and the econometrics literature on partial identification under misclassification (Molinari 2008; Kreider et al. 2012). The CSS annotation quality literature (beyond Ziems et al. 2024) includes Bender et al. 2021 on annotation artifacts, Pavlick & Kwiatkowski 2019 on disagreement as signal, and Plank et al. 2022 on label variation—all relevant to the claim that LLM annotation error structure is approximately uniform across subgroups.\n\n- **Budget analysis framing.** The n≥750 expert label requirement is presented as a finding but not contextualized against the cost of the annotation tasks involved. For CSS researchers using LLMs *to avoid* expensive human annotation, a 750-label validation requirement may be prohibitive, which would strengthen the negative finding further—but this implication is not drawn.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **K>2 bound:** Can you provide a formal decomposition of the bias for K>2 that isolates the Δπ×Δτ first-order term and characterizes when higher-order interactions dominate? The tightness ratio degrading to 3.8 at K=8 raises the question of whether the bound has any practical utility for multi-class modifiers.\n\n2. **PPCI regime map:** For what specific data-generating conditions (in terms of error structure) does EC-HTE strictly outperform PPCI? Is there an experimental setting where you can demonstrate this cleanly, rather than characterizing them as \"complementary\"?\n\n3. **Generalizability of the negative finding:** Can you report the max/min misclassification ratio across at least 2–3 additional annotation tasks with different subgroup structures (e.g., demographic subgroups vs. text-based subgroups, different label types)? The single-dataset negative finding is the empirical centerpiece of the paper, and its scope is unclear.\n\n4. **Hierarchical Bayesian EC-HTE uncertainty propagation:** Does the HB partial pooling produce posterior uncertainty estimates over the corrected CATE that propagate annotation uncertainty into the downstream causal estimate? If so, how does this compare to bootstrap-based uncertainty quantification in the global correction baseline?\n\n5. **Causal forest underperformance:** The EC-HTE leaf-level correction underperforms global (RMSE 0.038 vs. 0.036). Is this because leaf-level subgroups are misaligned with annotation subgroups, or because leaf-level sample sizes are insufficient for the HB model? Understanding the mechanism is important for knowing whether this is a fundamental limitation or an implementation choice.\n\n---\n\n### Missing Related Work and Comparisons\n\n**Measurement error / misclassification:**\n- Molinari (2008, ECMA): Partial identification under misclassification—most relevant to the K>2 case where the bound loosens.\n- Kreider et al. (2012, JASA): Bounding treatment effects with misclassified treatment.\n- Chen, Hong, Tamer (2005, REStud): Measurement error in binary regressors—directly addresses the K=2 case with econometric rigor.\n\n**HTE with covariate error:**\n- Kennedy et al. (2020): Doubly-robust nonparametric regression with mismeasured covariates—not cited, overlaps with the correction target.\n- Semenova & Chernozhukov (2021) on CATE estimation with nuisance error—relevant to how annotation error propagates in doubly robust estimators.\n\n**CSS annotation quality:**\n- Plank et al. (2022, ACL): Annotation disagreement as structured signal—relevant to whether LLM annotation heterogeneity is random or systematic.\n- Pavlick & Kwiatkowski (2019): Inherent disagreements as linguistic signal—challenges the assumption that annotation errors are purely noise.\n- Bender et al. (2021): Data annotation biases in NLP benchmarks.\n\n**Bayesian annotation models:**\n- Hovy et al. (2013): Item-response theory for annotation—more expressive than Dawid-Skene, could be an EC-HTE backbone alternative.\n- Rodrigues & Pereira (2018): Deep learning from crowds—extends D&S to neural annotator models, relevant if LLM annotation errors have structured dependence on text features.\n\n**Comparison gap:** No comparison to a simple heteroskedastic-error correction (e.g., reweighting by inverse predicted error rate) is provided. EC-HTE's complexity should be benchmarked against this trivial baseline before claiming the hierarchical Bayesian structure is necessary.\n\n---\n\n### Score and Justification\n\n**Score: 3 / 6 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe paper addresses a real and underexplored problem—the interaction between misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity in LLM-annotated causal studies—with a clean K=2 analytic result and commendably honest reporting. However, the submission has three compounding weaknesses that, taken together, fall short of the bar for a top venue:\n\n1. The K=2 bound is tight and useful; the K>2 extension is not, and the paper's scope implicitly covers multi-class modifiers without delivering comparable theoretical clarity.\n2. EC-HTE's novelty reduces to a known Dawid-Skene + matrix inversion pipeline; the hierarchical Bayesian wrapping adds practical robustness but not conceptual novelty. The paper does not sufficiently argue why this combination warrants independent publication rather than being presented as a methodological appendix to a broader study.\n3. The negative empirical finding—the paper's claimed centerpiece—rests on three LLMs evaluated on a single dataset. The r=−0.85 correlation over three points is insufficient to make the \"problem will diminish as LLMs improve\" claim, which is the paper's strongest practical takeaway.\n\nThe negative result has genuine value for the CSS community, and the paper is close to publishable. Revision should focus on expanding the empirical scope of the negative finding, formally characterizing the K>2 bound's practical limits, and resolving the PPCI overlap question with a clean regime map. With these changes, the paper could reasonably reach a 4 (Weak Accept).\n\n**Confidence: 4 / 5**\n\nConfident in the assessment of related work coverage and technical novelty evaluation. Moderate uncertainty about the internal details of the EC-HTE implementation and whether the hierarchical Bayesian uncertainty propagation adds value not captured in the summary."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a28e9590998bd5004 (use SendMessage with to: 'a28e9590998bd5004' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 19035\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 63528</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d4a6cb8-7b88-4571-bf20-c9431ac3715e","uuid":"bab8e861-5120-46db-a6b8-32d70c0980e2","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:35:21.008Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review. Your focus: **Novelty and Related Work**. Use a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"When Does Subgroup-Stratified Correction Matter for LLM-Annotated Heterogeneous Treatment Effects?\"\n\n**Core idea:** LLM annotations used as effect modifiers in HTE analysis have subgroup-varying misclassification rates. Applying global confusion-matrix correction may introduce systematic bias. The paper formalizes this bias, derives an analytic bound (bias ∝ Δπ × Δτ), proposes EC-HTE (hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction), and finds empirically that current frontier LLMs rarely trigger conditions where stratified correction matters.\n\n**Three contributions:**\n1. Analytic bound: |Bias_k^global| ≤ C · Δπ · Δτ for K=2 (R²=0.92), conservative for K>2 (R² drops to 0.68/0.52 for K=4/8)\n2. EC-HTE framework: HB Dirichlet partial pooling for per-subgroup confusion matrices + CATE correction via matrix inversion\n3. Negative empirical finding: current frontier LLMs show limited misclassification heterogeneity (max/min 1.10–1.18), making global correction sufficient in most cases\n\n## Related Work as Presented (§2)\n\n**Measurement error in causal inference:** Carroll et al. (2006), Gustafson (2004) on mismeasured covariates. Hu and Schennach (2008), Schennach (2016) on nonparametric identification under misclassification — population-level, not the specific interaction between misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity.\n\n**LLM annotation correction:** \n- PPI (Angelopoulos et al., 2023): valid inference combining LLM + small labeled data, assumes uniform correction\n- DSL (Egami et al., 2024): design-based guarantees for text-as-treatment\n- Mozer (2026): arm-specific rectifiers for differential prediction error, but ATE only\n- PPCI (Sui et al., 2026): kernel localization for conditional functionals, outcome-side measurement error\n\n**HTE methods:** Athey et al. (2019), Wager and Athey (2018) causal forests. ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression forests but not mismeasured effect modifier.\n\n**Bayesian annotation:** Dawid and Skene (1979), Paun et al. (2018) — applied to consensus labeling, not causal estimation.\n\n## Key Experimental Findings (for assessing claim novelty)\n\n- Bias bound: K=2 is exact and elegant (R²=0.92, tightness 1.15), but K>2 degrades significantly (tightness ratio 2.3–3.8). The Δπ×Δτ product is a first-order approximation for K>2.\n- EC-HTE vs PPCI: Methods are \"complementary but partially overlapping.\" PPCI achieves 55% bias reduction on covariate-side error via kernel localization, partially addressing EC-HTE's target problem.\n- Diagnostic criterion: AUC=0.70 — better than chance but insufficient for reliable use. Framed as \"conservative sufficient condition.\"\n- Negative result: All three frontier LLMs (GPT-4.1, Qwen3-235B, Claude Sonnet 4.6) show limited heterogeneity. Negative correlation r=−0.85 suggests problem diminishes as LLMs improve.\n- Budget: EC-HTE needs n≥750 expert labels (K=2 moderate) to outperform global correction.\n- Robustness: EC-HTE becomes worse than global at ≥25% subgroup noise or with misaligned subgroups.\n- Causal forest integration: EC-HTE leaf-level correction underperforms global correction (RMSE 0.038 vs 0.036).\n\n## 12 References in the Paper\nAngelopoulos et al. 2023 (PPI), Ashwin et al. 2025 (refugee data), Athey et al. 2019 (GRF), Bu et al. 2023 (ForestIV), Carroll et al. 2006 (measurement error), Dawid & Skene 1979 (annotator models), Egami et al. 2024 (DSL), Gustafson 2004 (measurement error), Hu & Schennach 2008, Mozer 2026, Paun et al. 2018, Schennach 2016, Sui et al. 2026 (PPCI), Wager & Athey 2018, Ziems et al. 2024.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: Is the bias formalization (Δπ×Δτ bound) genuinely new? Is EC-HTE technically novel beyond standard Bayesian confusion matrix modeling + matrix inversion? Is the negative finding surprising or already expected?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there missing baselines or related works? Especially in measurement error literature, HTE estimation, and CSS annotation quality.\n3. **Positioning**: Is the paper correctly positioned relative to PPI, DSL, PPCI, Mozer? Does the differentiation hold up given PPCI's partial effectiveness on covariate error?\n4. **Significance**: For the CL+CSS community — does this negative finding have sufficient practical value?\n5. **Framing quality**: Is the negative-result framing convincing? Does the paper deliver on what it promises?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for Authors\n- Missing Related Work or Comparisons\n- Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The paper is framed as a negative result — assess whether the negative finding itself constitutes a sufficient contribution.","agentId":"a28e9590998bd5004","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Review: \"When Does Subgroup-Stratified Correction Matter for LLM-Annotated Heterogeneous Treatment Effects?\"\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThe paper investigates whether subgroup-heterogeneous misclassification rates in LLM annotations introduce meaningful bias when estimating heterogeneous treatment effects (HTE), formalizing a bias bound proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity (Δπ) and CATE heterogeneity (Δτ), proposing a hierarchical Bayesian correction framework (EC-HTE), and ultimately reporting that current frontier LLMs rarely trigger conditions where stratified correction meaningfully outperforms global correction. The central contribution is a negative empirical finding grounded in a theoretical framework, positioned against the growing literature on LLM-assisted causal inference. While the bias formalization is clean and the empirical scope is reasonable, the technical novelty is modest, and the negative finding—though useful—risks being interpreted as a justification for inaction rather than a principled demarcation of when the problem matters.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Clean analytic result for K=2.** The |Bias_k^global| ≤ C · Δπ · Δτ bound is elegant and practically interpretable. An R²=0.92 tightness with ratio 1.15 means the bound is genuinely informative for the binary case, which is the most common case in CSS annotation tasks. This is the paper's strongest contribution.\n\n- **Precise problem formulation.** The paper identifies a specific gap—interaction between misclassification *heterogeneity* and CATE heterogeneity—that is distinct from existing measurement error literature (Carroll/Gustafson) focused on population-level error, and from PPI/PPCI which assume uniform or outcome-side error. The conceptual framing is tight and non-obvious.\n\n- **Honest reporting of negative results.** The authors do not oversell EC-HTE. Reporting that EC-HTE underperforms global correction on causal forest integration (RMSE 0.038 vs 0.036), degrades at ≥25% subgroup noise, and requires n≥750 expert labels is a level of transparency uncommon in ML venues. The negative correlation r=−0.85 between LLM quality and misclassification heterogeneity is a particularly useful calibration for the community.\n\n- **Practical diagnostic value.** The AUC=0.70 sufficient-condition criterion, even if imperfect, provides practitioners a pre-analysis check before committing to expensive stratified annotation. For CSS researchers deploying LLMs at scale, \"when does this even matter?\" is the right prior question.\n\n- **Correct gap identification relative to ForestIV.** ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression forests but does not address the mismeasured *effect modifier* setting. The distinction is correctly drawn and not trivial.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**Major:**\n\n- **K>2 bound degrades severely and this is underplayed.** The tightness ratio of 2.3–3.8 for K=4/8 (R² dropping to 0.68/0.52) means the bound is near-vacuous for multi-class effect modifiers. Many realistic CSS annotation tasks involve ordinal or multi-class labels (e.g., sentiment polarity, issue framing, political leaning). The paper implicitly positions itself as a general framework while delivering a tight result only for binary modifiers. This should be clearly foregrounded as a limitation, not buried. The claim that Δπ×Δτ is a \"first-order approximation for K>2\" requires theoretical justification—what are the higher-order terms, and when do they dominate?\n\n- **EC-HTE novelty is thin.** The framework combines two well-known components: (1) Dawid-Skene-style Dirichlet confusion matrix estimation (Dawid & Skene 1979, Paun et al. 2018) with partial pooling, and (2) CATE correction via confusion matrix inversion. Both components exist in the literature; their combination in a hierarchical Bayesian wrapper is an engineering contribution, not a conceptual one. The paper should more explicitly acknowledge this and argue why the combination creates emergent properties—e.g., uncertainty propagation across the annotation and estimation stages—that would not be achieved by applying each component sequentially.\n\n- **PPCI partial overlap is insufficiently resolved.** The paper concedes PPCI achieves 55% bias reduction on covariate-side error via kernel localization and is \"complementary but partially overlapping.\" This framing is evasive. The critical question is: under what conditions does EC-HTE strictly dominate PPCI, and vice versa? Without a formal or empirical regime map (e.g., varying the fraction of error that is covariate-side vs. effect-modifier-side), the reader cannot assess whether EC-HTE fills a genuine gap or a narrow residual one. This is not a minor positioning issue—it goes to the core claim of the paper's necessity.\n\n- **The negative finding may be dataset-specific rather than generalizable.** The empirical result that frontier LLMs show max/min ratios of 1.10–1.18 is reported for three LLMs on what appears to be a single refugee-policy dataset (Ashwin et al., 2025). The finding that misclassification heterogeneity is limited could reflect properties of this particular annotation task and subgroup structure rather than a general law. The r=−0.85 correlation is intriguing but estimated over three data points (three LLMs). Reporting this correlation as if it characterizes a trend is statistically fragile. Broader empirical validation across annotation tasks with known ground-truth structure (e.g., social science datasets with high annotator disagreement, political or medical text) is needed to make the negative finding a genuine contribution rather than a case study.\n\n**Minor:**\n\n- **Diagnostic criterion underperformance not fully addressed.** An AUC of 0.70 for the sufficient condition is labeled \"conservative,\" but no baseline is given. A random subgroup-stratification detector would yield AUC=0.50; the relevant comparison is to simpler heuristics (e.g., subgroup size imbalance, annotation confidence variance). Without this, it is unclear how much the diagnostic adds over simple data inspection.\n\n- **Reference list is thin and potentially incomplete.** Twelve references for a paper targeting a top venue is sparse. The measurement error literature has directly relevant work beyond Carroll/Gustafson/Hu/Schennach—specifically, Chen et al. (2008) on semiparametric efficiency with mismeasured regressors, and the econometrics literature on partial identification under misclassification (Molinari 2008; Kreider et al. 2012). The CSS annotation quality literature (beyond Ziems et al. 2024) includes Bender et al. 2021 on annotation artifacts, Pavlick & Kwiatkowski 2019 on disagreement as signal, and Plank et al. 2022 on label variation—all relevant to the claim that LLM annotation error structure is approximately uniform across subgroups.\n\n- **Budget analysis framing.** The n≥750 expert label requirement is presented as a finding but not contextualized against the cost of the annotation tasks involved. For CSS researchers using LLMs *to avoid* expensive human annotation, a 750-label validation requirement may be prohibitive, which would strengthen the negative finding further—but this implication is not drawn.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **K>2 bound:** Can you provide a formal decomposition of the bias for K>2 that isolates the Δπ×Δτ first-order term and characterizes when higher-order interactions dominate? The tightness ratio degrading to 3.8 at K=8 raises the question of whether the bound has any practical utility for multi-class modifiers.\n\n2. **PPCI regime map:** For what specific data-generating conditions (in terms of error structure) does EC-HTE strictly outperform PPCI? Is there an experimental setting where you can demonstrate this cleanly, rather than characterizing them as \"complementary\"?\n\n3. **Generalizability of the negative finding:** Can you report the max/min misclassification ratio across at least 2–3 additional annotation tasks with different subgroup structures (e.g., demographic subgroups vs. text-based subgroups, different label types)? The single-dataset negative finding is the empirical centerpiece of the paper, and its scope is unclear.\n\n4. **Hierarchical Bayesian EC-HTE uncertainty propagation:** Does the HB partial pooling produce posterior uncertainty estimates over the corrected CATE that propagate annotation uncertainty into the downstream causal estimate? If so, how does this compare to bootstrap-based uncertainty quantification in the global correction baseline?\n\n5. **Causal forest underperformance:** The EC-HTE leaf-level correction underperforms global (RMSE 0.038 vs. 0.036). Is this because leaf-level subgroups are misaligned with annotation subgroups, or because leaf-level sample sizes are insufficient for the HB model? Understanding the mechanism is important for knowing whether this is a fundamental limitation or an implementation choice.\n\n---\n\n### Missing Related Work and Comparisons\n\n**Measurement error / misclassification:**\n- Molinari (2008, ECMA): Partial identification under misclassification—most relevant to the K>2 case where the bound loosens.\n- Kreider et al. (2012, JASA): Bounding treatment effects with misclassified treatment.\n- Chen, Hong, Tamer (2005, REStud): Measurement error in binary regressors—directly addresses the K=2 case with econometric rigor.\n\n**HTE with covariate error:**\n- Kennedy et al. (2020): Doubly-robust nonparametric regression with mismeasured covariates—not cited, overlaps with the correction target.\n- Semenova & Chernozhukov (2021) on CATE estimation with nuisance error—relevant to how annotation error propagates in doubly robust estimators.\n\n**CSS annotation quality:**\n- Plank et al. (2022, ACL): Annotation disagreement as structured signal—relevant to whether LLM annotation heterogeneity is random or systematic.\n- Pavlick & Kwiatkowski (2019): Inherent disagreements as linguistic signal—challenges the assumption that annotation errors are purely noise.\n- Bender et al. (2021): Data annotation biases in NLP benchmarks.\n\n**Bayesian annotation models:**\n- Hovy et al. (2013): Item-response theory for annotation—more expressive than Dawid-Skene, could be an EC-HTE backbone alternative.\n- Rodrigues & Pereira (2018): Deep learning from crowds—extends D&S to neural annotator models, relevant if LLM annotation errors have structured dependence on text features.\n\n**Comparison gap:** No comparison to a simple heteroskedastic-error correction (e.g., reweighting by inverse predicted error rate) is provided. EC-HTE's complexity should be benchmarked against this trivial baseline before claiming the hierarchical Bayesian structure is necessary.\n\n---\n\n### Score and Justification\n\n**Score: 3 / 6 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe paper addresses a real and underexplored problem—the interaction between misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity in LLM-annotated causal studies—with a clean K=2 analytic result and commendably honest reporting. However, the submission has three compounding weaknesses that, taken together, fall short of the bar for a top venue:\n\n1. The K=2 bound is tight and useful; the K>2 extension is not, and the paper's scope implicitly covers multi-class modifiers without delivering comparable theoretical clarity.\n2. EC-HTE's novelty reduces to a known Dawid-Skene + matrix inversion pipeline; the hierarchical Bayesian wrapping adds practical robustness but not conceptual novelty. The paper does not sufficiently argue why this combination warrants independent publication rather than being presented as a methodological appendix to a broader study.\n3. The negative empirical finding—the paper's claimed centerpiece—rests on three LLMs evaluated on a single dataset. The r=−0.85 correlation over three points is insufficient to make the \"problem will diminish as LLMs improve\" claim, which is the paper's strongest practical takeaway.\n\nThe negative result has genuine value for the CSS community, and the paper is close to publishable. Revision should focus on expanding the empirical scope of the negative finding, formally characterizing the K>2 bound's practical limits, and resolving the PPCI overlap question with a clean regime map. With these changes, the paper could reasonably reach a 4 (Weak Accept).\n\n**Confidence: 4 / 5**\n\nConfident in the assessment of related work coverage and technical novelty evaluation. Moderate uncertainty about the internal details of the EC-HTE implementation and whether the hierarchical Bayesian uncertainty propagation adds value not captured in the summary."}],"totalDurationMs":63528,"totalTokens":19035,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7838,"cache_read_input_tokens":8114,"output_tokens":3080,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7838},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":3080,"cache_read_input_tokens":8114,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7838,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7838,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01W4kpbdgTJH3Rn3cMjeg6p7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have submitted their independent reviews. Now I need to execute Steps 2-4 of the roundtable:\n\n**Step 2: Cross-share** — Each reviewer reads the other's review\n**Step 3: Convergence** — Identify agreements, disagreements, resolve\n**Step 4: Synthesize** — Produce the final consolidated report\n\nBoth reviewers gave Score 3 (Weak Reject) with Confidence 4. Let me synthesize.\n\nKey agreements:\n1. K>2 bound degradation is a major weakness\n2. Coverage below nominal (83-91%) is problematic\n\n\n3. The negative finding has limited empirical scope across just three LLMs and roughly one dataset\n4. EC-HTE's novelty is modest, essentially combining Dawid-Skene with matrix inversion\n5. PPCI overlap remains insufficiently addressed\n6. Real-data evaluation lacks a validity anchor\n7. The budget requirement of n≥750 significantly limits practical applicability\n\nThe reviewers diverge somewhat in emphasis—R1 prioritizes the HB versus MLE performance gap and coverage failures, while R2 emphasizes the thin novelty and sparse related work coverage. Despite these different angles, both converge on a Weak Reject. Now I'll draft the consolidated report and submit it.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10302,"cache_read_input_tokens":54120,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10302},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d4a6cb8-7b88-4571-bf20-c9431ac3715e","uuid":"f4f5bde6-7343-4365-a2a2-a8a34f9c06ef"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01W4kpbdgTJH3Rn3cMjeg6p7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"两位 Reviewer 独立审稿完成，均给出 3/6（Weak Reject）。现在进入 Step 2-3 交叉讨论和收敛，然后 Step 4 综合报告。\n\n---\n\n**Roundtable 交叉分析：**\n\n**共识点：**\n1. K=2 bound 清晰有用，但 K>2 严重退化（tightness ratio 3.8），是核心理论弱点\n2. Coverage 系统性低于 nominal（83-91% vs 95%），未诊断根因\n3. 负面结果有价值但实证范围窄（3 个 LLM，基本一个 domain）\n4. EC-HTE 技术新颖性有限（Dawid-Skene + 矩阵反转的组合）\n5. PPCI 55% partial effectiveness 削弱了差异化叙事\n6. Real-data 无 ground truth，证据力弱\n7. 两位均给 3/6，Confidence 4\n\n**分歧点：**\n- R1 更关注方法论层面（HB coverage failure、HB 反而在 K=4 extreme 更差）\n- R2 更关注定位层面（novelty 薄、related work 不够、负面发现的泛化性）\n- 无根本性分歧，收敛为 Weak Reject\n\n现在生成综合报告并提交。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10302,"cache_read_input_tokens":54120,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10302},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d4a6cb8-7b88-4571-bf20-c9431ac3715e","uuid":"9f0dab7c-a71d-4ce7-98eb-d879395f1e1d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01W4kpbdgTJH3Rn3cMjeg6p7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HAfu3rMdv2mzKnxPjTnY5Z","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report\n\n**Paper:** \"When Does Subgroup-Stratified Correction Matter for LLM-Annotated Heterogeneous Treatment Effects?\"\n\n**Review mode:** Full (2 Reviewers, 4-step roundtable)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Paper Summary\n\nThe paper investigates whether subgroup-stratified confusion-matrix correction is necessary when LLM annotations serve as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect (HTE) estimation. It formalizes the bias mechanism from applying global (pooled) correction under heterogeneous LLM misclassification rates, derives an analytic bound (bias ∝ Δπ × Δτ), proposes EC-HTE—a hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction framework—and evaluates it across synthetic, semi-synthetic, and real-data settings with three frontier LLMs (GPT-4.1, Qwen3-235B, Claude Sonnet 4.6). The central finding is negative: current frontier LLMs exhibit limited misclassification heterogeneity (max/min accuracy ratios 1.10–1.18), global correction introduces modest additional bias (amplification 1.05–1.15×), and EC-HTE provides only marginal improvements (22–38% relative bias reduction) at substantial cost in expert labels, computation, and sensitivity to subgroup specification.\n\n**Claimed contributions:** (1) Analytic bias bound for K=2 (tight, R²=0.92) and conservative sufficient condition for K>2; (2) EC-HTE framework with Dirichlet partial pooling; (3) Negative empirical finding that stratified correction is rarely necessary for current frontier LLMs.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nAll 11 imagined experiments from the design document are covered in the paper:\n\n| Experiment | Covered | Section |\n|---|---|---|\n| exp-001: HB vs MLE Synthetic | ✓ | §5.1.1 |\n| exp-002: Bias Bound Verification | ✓ | §3 |\n| exp-003: Semi-Synthetic Benchmark | ✓ | §5.2.1 |\n| exp-004: Ashwin Real-Data | ✓ | §5.2.2 |\n| exp-005: Diagnostic Criterion | ✓ | §5.3.2 |\n| exp-006: Budget Sensitivity | ✓ | §5.1.2 |\n| exp-007: PPCI Comparison | ✓ | §5.3.1 |\n| exp-008: Multi-LLM | ✓ | §5.2.3 |\n| exp-009: Adaptive Allocation | ✓ | §5.4.2 |\n| exp-010: Robustness | ✓ | §5.4.1 |\n| exp-011: Causal Forest | ✓ | §5.4.3 |\n\nCoverage is complete. The paper faithfully reports all experiments including those with unfavorable results (exp-010 robustness degradation, exp-011 causal forest underperformance).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Reviewer Reports\n\n### Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Clean bias decomposition (Proposition 1) with practically interpretable Δπ × Δτ product\n- Honest quantified negative result with concrete effect sizes (amplification 1.05–1.15×, bias reductions 22–38%)\n- Comprehensive 11-experiment coverage spanning synthetic-to-real progression\n- Appropriate MCMC diagnostics (R̂_max=1.005, ESS≥210)\n- Diagnostic criterion has immediate practical value as pre-analysis screening tool\n\n**Major Weaknesses:**\n1. **K>2 bound catastrophic degradation.** R² drops from 0.92 (K=2) → 0.68 (K=4) → 0.52 (K=8); tightness ratio reaches 3.80×. For multi-category effect modifiers (common in CSS), the bound provides near-vacuous guidance. The paper should derive a tighter multi-category bound or explicitly restrict claims to K=2.\n2. **Systematic coverage failure (83–91% vs. 95%) undiagnosed.** For a framework whose value partly rests on uncertainty quantification (HB over MLE), unexplained miscoverage is a methodological red flag. No root cause analysis provided—is this prior misspecification, inverse-matrix-step non-Gaussian posterior geometry, or structural?\n3. **Real-data evaluation (exp-004) lacks validity anchor.** No ground-truth CATEs → cannot distinguish bias reduction from overcorrection. No partial-identification bounds, sensitivity analysis, or placebo test offered.\n4. **HB underperforms MLE at K=4 extreme regime (+5.5% RMSE) with 45× runtime.** Partial pooling hurts in exactly the regime where stratified correction should matter most—suggesting harmful shrinkage under genuine heterogeneity. Inadequately explained.\n\n**Minor Weaknesses:**\n- Budget crossover (n≥750 for K=2 moderate; >2000 for K≥4) not translated into practitioner-facing decision guidance\n- Adaptive allocation (12% RMSE improvement at n=250) may be undersold as a practical contribution\n- Subgroup misalignment frequency in real settings not characterized\n- Diagnostic AUC=0.70 underanalyzed—no breakdown by K, n, or Δτ magnitude\n\n### Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty and Related Work\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject) | Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Precise gap identification: interaction between misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity is genuinely unexplored\n- Clean K=2 analytic result with R²=0.92 tightness\n- Honest reporting of unfavorable results (causal forest underperformance, robustness degradation)\n- Correct differentiation from ForestIV (mismeasured effect modifier vs. mismeasured covariate)\n- Practical diagnostic value for CSS researchers\n\n**Major Weaknesses:**\n1. **EC-HTE novelty is thin.** Combines Dawid-Skene-style Dirichlet confusion matrix estimation with CATE correction via matrix inversion—both well-established. The hierarchical Bayesian wrapping adds robustness, not conceptual novelty. Paper should explicitly argue why the combination creates emergent properties.\n2. **PPCI partial overlap insufficiently resolved.** PPCI achieves 55% bias reduction on covariate-side error (0.45 vs. 1.0 no-correction). The paper calls this \"complementary\" but provides no regime map showing when EC-HTE strictly dominates PPCI. This goes to the necessity of the proposed method.\n3. **Negative finding may be dataset-specific.** Three LLMs on essentially one domain (refugee interviews/political text). The r=−0.85 correlation is estimated over three data points—statistically fragile. Broader validation across annotation tasks needed.\n4. **K>2 bound degradation underplayed.** Implicit positioning as a general framework while delivering tight results only for binary modifiers.\n\n**Minor Weaknesses:**\n- Reference list is thin (12 references). Missing: Molinari (2008) on partial identification under misclassification, Chen/Hong/Tamer (2005) on mismeasured binary regressors, Hovy et al. (2013) on IRT annotation models, Plank et al. (2022) on annotation disagreement\n- No comparison to simple heteroskedastic-error correction (inverse predicted error rate reweighting)\n- Budget requirement (n≥750) not contextualized against CSS annotation cost structures\n- Diagnostic AUC=0.70 not benchmarked against simpler heuristics\n\n---\n\n## 4. Roundtable Synthesis\n\n### 4.1 Points of Convergence\n\nBoth reviewers converge on Weak Reject (3/6) with high confidence (4/5). The key agreements:\n\n1. **K=2 bound is the paper's strongest contribution**—clean, tight, practically useful. But the K>2 extension is inadequate for a general-purpose contribution.\n2. **Coverage failure is a serious unresolved issue** that undermines EC-HTE's claimed advantage over MLE in uncertainty quantification.\n3. **Negative finding has genuine value** for CSS practitioners but **empirical scope is too narrow** (3 LLMs, ~1 domain) to support the generalized claim.\n4. **EC-HTE's technical novelty is limited**—the combination of known components does not clearly create emergent value beyond what practitioners could assemble from existing tools.\n5. **PPCI overlap weakens the differentiation story**—the paper's positioning as filling a gap left by existing methods is partially undercut by PPCI's unexpected partial effectiveness on covariate-side error.\n6. **Real-data evaluation provides limited evidence** without a validity anchor.\n\n### 4.2 Points of Divergence (Resolved)\n\n- R1 emphasized methodological concerns (coverage, HB vs MLE tradeoff); R2 emphasized positioning concerns (novelty, related work, generalizability). These are complementary rather than contradictory—both point to the same conclusion that the paper is close but not yet at the acceptance bar.\n- No disagreement on the overall assessment.\n\n### 4.3 Actionable Revision Recommendations\n\n**High priority (likely needed for acceptance):**\n1. Diagnose and address the coverage failure—at minimum identify root cause; ideally provide bootstrap-corrected intervals as an alternative\n2. Expand the negative finding's empirical scope: additional annotation tasks, more diverse LLMs (including weaker open-source models), different subgroup structures\n3. Provide a formal or empirical regime map for EC-HTE vs. PPCI showing strict dominance regions\n4. Tighten the K>2 bound or clearly restrict claims to K=2 with a frank discussion of multi-class limitations\n\n**Medium priority:**\n5. Add missing related work (Molinari 2008, Chen/Hong/Tamer 2005, Plank et al. 2022, Hovy et al. 2013)\n6. Benchmark diagnostic criterion against simpler heuristics\n7. Explain HB underperformance at K=4 extreme regime—investigate whether hyperprior induces harmful shrinkage\n8. Add practitioner-facing decision table (K × heterogeneity regime × required n)\n\n**Low priority:**\n9. Consider elevating adaptive allocation as a practical contribution\n10. Characterize subgroup misalignment frequency in real applications\n\n---\n\n## 5. Overall Assessment\n\n**Consolidated Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Headline:** The paper identifies a genuine and underexplored problem—the interaction between misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity in LLM-annotated causal studies—and provides a clean K=2 analytic characterization with commendably honest negative results. However, the submission falls short of the top-venue bar due to: (1) the analytic bound losing most diagnostic value for K>2 without adequate resolution, (2) unexplained systematic coverage failure in the proposed HB framework, (3) thin technical novelty in EC-HTE itself, and (4) a negative empirical finding whose generalizability rests on too narrow a base (3 LLMs, 1 primary domain).\n\nThe paper is clearly above the rejection threshold and has meaningful practical value for the CSS community. With the recommended revisions—particularly expanding the empirical scope, diagnosing the coverage issue, and resolving the PPCI overlap—this could reach Findings-level acceptance. The negative-result framing is well-executed and the experimental honesty is exemplary; these are strengths that revision should preserve.\n\n**Estimated venue fit:** Below the bar for a top ML venue main conference track at this stage. Competitive for a workshop or Findings track after targeted revision.","project":"ec_hte_llm_annotation","tier":"worst","dream_id":"20260520T194535","overall_score":"3/6 Weak Reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10302,"cache_read_input_tokens":54120,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10302},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7d4a6cb8-7b88-4571-bf20-c9431ac3715e","uuid":"431c49ec-7943-4ef2-afba-ba5297651f90"}
